First, compute the photon energy for the 632.8 nm laser emission:

\[
E_{\text{photon}} = \frac{hc}{\lambda}
\]

Using \(h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34}\ \text{J·s}\), \(c = 3.00 \times 10^8\ \text{m/s}\), and \(\lambda = 632.8\ \text{nm} = 6.328 \times 10^{-7}\ \text{m}\):

\[
E_{\text{photon}} = \frac{(6.626 \times 10^{-34})(3.00 \times 10^8)}{6.328 \times 10^{-7}}
\]

\[
E_{\text{photon}} \approx \frac{1.9878 \times 10^{-25}}{6.328 \times 10^{-7}}
\approx 3.14 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

This is the energy difference between the 3s and 2p levels:

\[
E_{3s} - E_{2p} \approx 3.14 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

Given \(E_{2p} = 15.2 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}\), the 3s level energy is:

\[
E_{3s} = E_{2p} + E_{\text{photon}}
\approx 15.2 \times 10^{-19} + 3.14 \times 10^{-19}
= 18.34 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

Assuming no loss in the helium-neon transition, the pumping energy required must raise the neon atom from the ground state to the 3s level. Since the problem gives the 2p level energy and the transition energy, the required pumping energy is the energy of the 3s level:

\[
E_{\text{pump}} = E_{3s} \approx 18.34 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

This matches option C, \(18.32 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}\), within rounding.

Final selected option:

(C) \(18.32 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}\)

